    deck: "Getting around",
    body: `
<p>English puts "can", "want" and "must" before the verb. Thai does that with "want" and "must", but "can" goes at the end. That one difference causes most of the mistakes in this lesson.</p>
<h3>Want: อยาก + verb</h3>
<p><b>อยาก</b> <i>yàak</i> goes before a verb: "want to go", "want to eat". The verb is needed: Thai does not say "want the beach". For "don't want", put <b>ไม่</b> in front: <b>ไม่อยากไป</b>, I don't want to go. To turn down an offer of help, <b>ไม่ต้อง</b> with ครับ or ค่ะ does the job; it comes up below.</p>
{{ex:อยากไปทะเล|yàak bpai thá-lee|I want to go to the beach.}}
<p>To want a thing, use <b>อยากได้</b> <i>yàak dâai</i>, literally "want to get".</p>
{{ex:ฉันอยากได้กระเป๋าใบนี้|chǎn yàak dâai grà-bpǎo bai níi|I want this bag. (female speaker)}}
<h3>Can: verb + ได้</h3>
<p>English says "I can speak Thai". Thai says "speak Thai can": <b>ได้</b> <i>dâai</i> comes after the verb and what follows it. For "can't", put <b>ไม่</b> before it: <b>ไม่ได้</b>.</p>
{{ex:ผมพูดภาษาไทยได้นิดหน่อย|phǒm phûut phaa-sǎa thai dâai nít-nàwi|I can speak a little Thai. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ไปไม่ได้|bpai mâi dâai|I can't go.}}
<p>For skills you learned, like driving or swimming, you will also hear <b>เป็น</b> in the same place: <b>ขับรถไม่เป็น</b>, "I don't know how to drive".</p>
<p>Watch the position. <b>ได้</b> before a verb means "got to": <b>ได้ไป</b> is "got to go, had the chance to go", not "can go".</p>
<h3>Asking: ได้ไหม</h3>
<p>Add <b>ได้ไหม</b> <i>dâai mǎi</i> at the end to ask "can you …?" or "can I …?". Answer with <b>ได้</b> (yes) or <b>ไม่ได้</b> (no), never with ใช่.</p>
{{ex:ว่ายน้ำได้ไหม|wâai-náam dâai mǎi|Can you swim?}}
{{ex:รอที่นี่ได้ไหม|raaw thîi-nîi dâai mǎi|Can you wait here?}}
<h3>Must: ต้อง + verb</h3>
<p><b>ต้อง</b> <i>dtâwng</i> goes before the verb, like "have to". <b>ไม่ต้อง</b> means "you don't have to", a handy "no need" when someone offers help.</p>
{{ex:ต้องไปแล้ว|dtâwng bpai láaeo|I have to go now.}}
{{ex:ต้องเปลี่ยนรถไหม|dtâwng bplìian rót mǎi|Do I have to change?}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting "can" first, as in English. <b>ได้พูดภาษาไทย</b> for "I can speak Thai" → <b>พูดภาษาไทยได้</b>. ได้ goes after the verb.</div>
`,
