    id: "feelings",
    title: "Feelings and health",
    blurb: "Say how you feel, say what hurts and that you are sick, and get help at a pharmacy.",
    deck: "Health and feelings",
    body: `
<p>English says "I am tired", "I have a headache", "I have a cold". Thai sorts these differently: feelings need no verb at all, pains need no "have", and only illnesses take <b>เป็น</b> <i>bpen</i>.</p>
<h3>Feelings: no เป็น</h3>
<p>Feelings are describing verbs, like <b>หิว</b> (hungry). Put them straight after the person.</p>
{{ex:ผมเหนื่อย|phǒm nùueai|I'm tired. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ฉันดีใจมาก|chǎn dii-jai mâak|I'm very glad. (female speaker)}}
<p>Others to know: <b>เสียใจ</b> <i>sǐia-jai</i> (sad, sorry about something), <b>เครียด</b> <i>khrîiat</i> (stressed), <b>ง่วงนอน</b> <i>ngûuang-naawn</i> (sleepy). <b>ไม่สบาย</b> <i>mâi-sà-baai</i>, literally "not comfortable", is the everyday word for "sick".</p>
<h3>What hurts: ปวด and เจ็บ</h3>
<p>English says "I have a headache". Thai says "ache head": <b>ปวด</b> <i>bpùuat</i> + the body part, with no "have". <b>ปวด</b> is a dull ache inside; <b>เจ็บ</b> <i>jèp</i> is a sharp or sore pain, like a sore throat or a cut.</p>
{{ex:ปวดหัว|bpùuat-hǔua|headache; my head hurts}}
{{ex:ปวดท้อง|bpùuat-tháawng|stomachache}}
{{ex:เจ็บคอ|jèp khaaw|sore throat}}
<h3>Illnesses: เป็น + illness</h3>
<p>Of feelings, pains and illnesses, only illnesses take <b>เป็น</b>: you "are" a cold, you "are" a fever. A fever is <b>ไข้</b> <i>khâi</i>, with a falling tone; with a low tone, <b>ไข่</b> is an egg. You will also hear <b>มีไข้</b>. <b>ร้อน</b> (hot) alone means hot weather or a hot stove, so for a fever say เป็นไข้ or มีไข้.</p>
{{ex:ผมเป็นหวัด|phǒm bpen wàt|I have a cold. (male speaker)}}
{{ex:ฉันเป็นไข้|chǎn bpen khâi|I have a fever. (female speaker)}}
<h3>Allergies: แพ้</h3>
<p><b>แพ้</b> <i>pháae</i> + the thing means "allergic to". It is worth knowing before you order food.</p>
{{ex:แพ้กุ้ง|pháae gûng|I'm allergic to shrimp.}}
<h3>At the pharmacy</h3>
<p>Pharmacies, <b>ร้านขายยา</b> <i>ráan-khǎai-yaa</i> ("shop selling medicine"), are everywhere, and pharmacists help with small problems. Say what is wrong, then ask <b>มียาไหม</b>: "do you have medicine?".</p>
{{ex:ร้านขายยาอยู่ใกล้ไหม|ráan-khǎai-yaa yùu glâi mǎi|Is the pharmacy near?}}
{{ex:มียาไหมคะ|mii yaa mǎi khá|Do you have any medicine? (female speaker)}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> adding เป็น to a feeling. <b>ผมเป็นเหนื่อย</b> → <b>ผมเหนื่อย</b>. Feelings take no เป็น; only illnesses do: <b>ผมเป็นหวัด</b>.</div>
`,
